    {
      "id": "physical_theory",
      "name": "01  DIFFERENTIAL EQUATION",
      "width": 1460,
      "height": 991,
      "controls": [
        {
          "id": "theoryHeader",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "01  WHY A CAPACITOR CHARGES EXPONENTIALLY",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 12,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 58,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": false,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "theoryIntro",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "Follow the physics: R limits current, capacitor current changes voltage, and the voltage difference shrinks as the capacitor approaches its new final value.",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 79,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 65,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": true,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "physics1",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "1. KIRCHHOFF’S VOLTAGE LAW",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 160,
          "width": 685,
          "height": 176,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "For VIN → R → capacitor → ground: VIN = VR + VC. Ohm’s law gives VR = R·i, while the capacitor current is i = C·dVC/dt.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "physics2",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "2. THE DIFFERENTIAL EQUATION",
          "x": 729,
          "y": 160,
          "width": 707,
          "height": 176,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Substitute current: RC·dVC/dt + VC = VIN. For a constant input level after the step, this is a first-order linear differential equation.",
            "tone": "Information"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "physicsEq1",
          "type": "equation",
          "text": "Physical law in time-domain form",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 351,
          "width": 1414,
          "height": 112,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "source": "RC\\,\\frac{dv_C}{dt}+v_C=v_{in}",
            "pointSize": 21,
            "titleVisible": true,
            "borderVisible": true
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "physics3",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "3. THE HOMOGENEOUS SOLUTION",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 478,
          "width": 685,
          "height": 175,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Let u=VC−Vfinal. Then du/dt = −u/(RC). Separating variables and integrating gives ln|u| = −t/(RC)+constant.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "physics4",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "4. THE EXPONENTIAL STEP RESPONSE",
          "x": 729,
          "y": 478,
          "width": 707,
          "height": 175,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "VC(t)=Vfinal+(Vinitial−Vfinal)exp(−t/τ), where τ=RC. This equation describes charge AND discharge; only the endpoints change.",
            "tone": "Information"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "physicsEq2",
          "type": "equation",
          "text": "General response to a constant-voltage step",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 667,
          "width": 1414,
          "height": 113,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "source": "v_C(t)=V_\\infty+(v_C(0)-V_\\infty)e^{-t/\\tau},\\quad\\tau=RC",
            "pointSize": 19,
            "titleVisible": true,
            "borderVisible": true
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "physics5",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "5. WHAT DOES ONE TIME CONSTANT MEAN?",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 794,
          "width": 685,
          "height": 172,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "After 1τ the remaining error is e^−1 ≈ 36.79%; the voltage has traveled 63.21% toward its new level. At 5τ it is 99.33% settled.",
            "tone": "Success"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "physics6",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "6. CONNECT TO THE AC MEASUREMENT",
          "x": 729,
          "y": 794,
          "width": 707,
          "height": 172,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "The same equation predicts H(jω)=1/(1+jωRC), and the −3 dB frequency fc=1/(2πRC). We can measure τ in time and fc in frequency independently.",
            "tone": "Success"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
