    {
      "id": "method63",
      "name": "06  THE 63.2% METHOD",
      "width": 1460,
      "height": 950,
      "controls": [
        {
          "id": "crossHeader",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "06  ONE TIME CONSTANT  /  THE 63.2% METHOD",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 12,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 58,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": false,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cross1",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 1 — FIND BOTH PLATEAUS",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 88,
          "width": 688,
          "height": 171,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "In a full charging or discharging interval identify the starting voltage V0 and the final steady voltage V∞. With a ±0.5 V square wave, the final value alternates high and low.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cross2",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 2 — MEASURE THE MOVEMENT",
          "x": 730,
          "y": 88,
          "width": 708,
          "height": 171,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "The change is ΔV = V∞−V0. At t=τ, the capacitor has made (1−e⁻¹)=63.212% of this movement, regardless of whether ΔV is positive or negative.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "crossEquation",
          "type": "equation",
          "text": "How to locate one time constant on any step",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 279,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 135,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "source": "v_C(\\tau)=V_\\infty+(V_0-V_\\infty)e^{-1}=V_0+0.63212(V_\\infty-V_0)",
            "pointSize": 17,
            "titleVisible": true,
            "borderVisible": true
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cross3",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 3 — FIND THE SAMPLE CROSSING",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 430,
          "width": 688,
          "height": 181,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Locate the 63.2% threshold in the sampled CH2 waveform, interpolate between adjacent samples, and subtract the CH1 input threshold crossing time. This is a graphical estimate of τ.",
            "tone": "Information"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cross4",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 4 — WHY LEAST SQUARES CAN BE BETTER",
          "x": 730,
          "y": 430,
          "width": 708,
          "height": 181,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "The crossing method uses a single threshold and is sensitive to noise, input-edge slew, and uncertain plateaus. A fit uses hundreds of sampled voltages and estimates asymptote as well as τ.",
            "tone": "Information"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "crossEquation2",
          "type": "equation",
          "text": "The response after 5 time constants",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 631,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 103,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "source": "1-e^{-5}=0.993262\\approx99.33\\%",
            "pointSize": 22,
            "titleVisible": true,
            "borderVisible": true
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cross5",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STUDENT EXERCISE",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 752,
          "width": 688,
          "height": 170,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Use 980 Ω and 571 nF. Calculate τ, 5τ and fc; then compare your 63.2% estimate with the least-squares τ from Page 05. Why can those values differ even when the RC model is correct?",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "cross6",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "METHOD NOTE — NOT FULLY INDEPENDENT",
          "x": 730,
          "y": 752,
          "width": 708,
          "height": 170,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Our 63.2% calculation uses the fitted asymptote and extrapolates the fitted amplitude back to the CH1 edge instant. The sampled threshold-crossing time is then interpolated. This is a useful cross-check, not an independent calibration.",
            "tone": "Warning"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "least_squares",
      "name": "07  FIT THEORY + DERIVATION",
      "width": 1460,
      "height": 1005,
      "controls": [
        {
          "id": "derivationHeader",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "07  EXPONENTIAL LEAST-SQUARES  /  ONE NONLINEAR PARAMETER",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 12,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 58,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": false,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "derivationIntro",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "We do not simply take the logarithm of measured voltage: log transformation can amplify errors and needs a known final value. Instead we fit the original voltage samples directly.",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 81,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 70,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": true,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "derivationEq1",
          "type": "equation",
          "text": "A general exponential model with three unknown parameters",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 169,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 110,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "source": "y_i=V_\\infty+A\\,e^{-t_i/\\tau}+\\varepsilon_i",
            "pointSize": 21,
            "titleVisible": true,
            "borderVisible": true
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "derive1",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "1 — UNKNOWN PARAMETERS",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 296,
          "width": 686,
          "height": 184,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "The desired τ appears inside the exponential (nonlinear). However for ANY fixed trial τ, the final voltage V∞ and coefficient A multiply known basis functions and are linear least-squares unknowns.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "derive2",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "2 — DEFINE A TRIAL BASIS",
          "x": 729,
          "y": 296,
          "width": 709,
          "height": 184,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "At trial τ: E[i]=exp(−t[i]/τ). Minimize SSE=Σ[y[i]−V∞−A·E[i]]² over V∞ and A. This is a two-column regression matrix X=[1,E] and target vector y.",
            "tone": "Information"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "derivationEq2",
          "type": "equation",
          "text": "Solve the inner linear least-squares problem exactly",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 497,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 124,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "source": "A=\\frac{\\Sigma Ey-(\\Sigma E)(\\Sigma y)/N}{\\Sigma E^2-(\\Sigma E)^2/N},\\quad V_\\infty=\\frac{\\Sigma y-A\\Sigma E}{N}",
            "pointSize": 16,
            "titleVisible": true,
            "borderVisible": true
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "derive3",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "3 — SEARCH ONLY OVER τ",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 639,
          "width": 686,
          "height": 180,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Evaluate SSE for 57 logarithmically spaced candidate τ values. Pick the lowest SSE neighborhood. Refine it using golden-section minimization in log(τ). No black-box optimizer is required.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "derive4",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "4 — REPEAT ON BOTH EDGE DIRECTIONS",
          "x": 729,
          "y": 639,
          "width": 709,
          "height": 180,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "Determine transitions from CH1, keep complete half-cycles, and fit CH2 separately for each RISE and FALL. Compute τrise, τfall and mean τ; inspect residuals for mismatch.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "derive5",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "5 — FIT QUALITY AND INTERPRETATION",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 837,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 150,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "For each transition, RMS residual=√(SSE/N) in volts. R²=1−SSE/SST. A low residual means the exponential describes the captured waveform; it does NOT prove the AD3 or RC components are calibrated.",
            "tone": "Warning"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "source_walk",
      "name": "08  ALGORITHM WALKTHROUGH",
      "width": 1460,
      "height": 981,
      "controls": [
        {
          "id": "sourceHeader",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "08  READ THE EXACT JAVASCRIPT  /  RCStepAnalysis.mjs",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 12,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 58,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": false,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "sourceIntro",
          "type": "label",
          "text": "These snippets are taken from the implementation saved INSIDE this .wfpanel. Open Libraries → RCStepAnalysis.mjs to inspect, edit, and test the full source.",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 80,
          "width": 1416,
          "height": 72,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "textFormat": "Plain Text",
            "wordWrap": true,
            "borderVisible": true,
            "cornerRadius": 10
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "source1",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 1 — DETECT CH1 EDGES",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 171,
          "width": 684,
          "height": 186,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "edgesFromInput(ch1, rateHz, fHz)\nFind 10% and 90% plateaus; threshold at their midpoint.\nIdentify rising/falling CH1 crossings; enforce minimum spacing.\nReject weak/noisy square-wave inputs.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "source2",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 2 — FIND COMPLETE HALF-CYCLES",
          "x": 731,
          "y": 171,
          "width": 707,
          "height": 186,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "for (let k=0; k<edges.length-1; k++) {\n  const first=edge.index+3, last=next.index-3;\n  ... fitTransition(ch2, first, last, rateHz, direction);\n}\nWe exclude partial intervals and a few edge samples.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "source3",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 3 — BUILD EXPONENTIAL BASIS",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 376,
          "width": 684,
          "height": 187,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "const e=Math.exp(-(i-first)/(rateHz*tau));\nn++; sumE+=e; sumEE+=e*e;\nsumY+=y; sumEY+=e*y;\nNo arbitrary assumed final capacitor voltage is needed.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "source4",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 4 — SOLVE FOR FINAL VOLTAGE + A",
          "x": 731,
          "y": 376,
          "width": 707,
          "height": 187,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "const ssE=sumEE-sumE*sumE/n;\nconst amplitude=(sumEY-sumE*sumY/n)/ssE;\nconst finalV=(sumY-amplitude*sumE)/n;\nThe fit is linear in these two parameters when tau is fixed.",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "source5",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 5 — MINIMIZE SSE OVER TAU",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 582,
          "width": 684,
          "height": 187,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "const L=Math.log(minTau), H=Math.log(maxTau);\n// Coarse logarithmic search, then golden-section refinement.\n// Each trial calls solveAtTau(...); no external solver.\nreturn {...fitted, first,last,direction};",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "source6",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 6 — DERIVE RC + CUTOFF",
          "x": 731,
          "y": 582,
          "width": 707,
          "height": 187,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "const riseTau=avg(rises,'tauS');\nconst fallTau=avg(falls,'tauS');\nconst tau=avg(transitions,'tauS');\nconst fc=1/(2*Math.PI*tau);",
            "tone": "Procedure"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "source7",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "STEP 7 — CHECK RESIDUALS",
          "x": 22,
          "y": 787,
          "width": 684,
          "height": 169,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "const pred=finalV+amplitude*Math.exp(-t/tau);\nconst diff=samples[i]-pred;\nsse+=diff*diff;\nrmseV=Math.sqrt(sse/n);\nCompare CH2 measured vs fitted curve and plot residual.",
            "tone": "Information"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "source8",
          "type": "instruction_card",
          "text": "BOUNDARIES YOU SHOULD UNDERSTAND",
          "x": 731,
          "y": 787,
          "width": 707,
          "height": 169,
          "value": 0,
          "min": 0,
          "max": 100,
          "events": {},
          "properties": {
            "body": "The fit assumes first-order RC behavior and a square-wave stimulus. CH1 has a finite edge slope. Scope sample noise and offsets can bias τ. A single RC model will not explain ringing, nonlinear elements, or clipping.",
            "tone": "Warning"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
